# Phase 3 read-only monitor uses the public Sepolia RPC by default and does not load this file.
# phase3_auto.py --live uses the existing DRY_RUN=false, RPC_URL and operator PRIVATE_KEY.
# Add --with-spot to include the registered single-pool spot vault with the same operator.
# Spot size is the smaller of its on-chain f564 cap and balance.
# Its public head/borrower reads use optional PHASE3_RPC_URL. Default execution is simulation.
# Phase 3 vault: explicit deploy/submit commands use RPC_URL and the operator PRIVATE_KEY.
# Submit requires DRY_RUN=false. The address comes from logs/phase3/deployment.json.
# See docs/PHASE3_VAULT.md for the enrolled owner, on-chain caps and role setup.
# Optional process environment: PHASE3_RPC_URL (HTTP), WATCHDOG_WEBHOOK_URL (Discord).
# Start Phase 2 with python main.py. See docs/EVENT_CONTRACT_SETUP.md.
# Local live senders check both halt markers and execution ledgers under one operator lock.
# Services may resume their own inventory; manual swaps/deployments require both ledgers flat.
# RPC endpoint: Infura or Alchemy, either works (the bot checks it is Sepolia, not who serves it).
#   Infura:  app.infura.io -> your API key -> Sepolia -> https://sepolia.infura.io/v3/YOUR_INFURA_API_KEY
#   Alchemy: app.alchemy.com -> Create app -> Chain Ethereum, Network Sepolia
#            -> https://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_ALCHEMY_API_KEY
# The URL contains the key, so treat it as a secret (the bot redacts it from its logs).
RPC_URL=https://sepolia.infura.io/v3/YOUR_INFURA_API_KEY
# free public fallback (rate limited, not for 24/7): https://ethereum-sepolia-rpc.publicnode.com
# Optional per-provider HTTP RPC rate cap. A Phase 2 scan reads roughly 700 bitmap
# words in the narrow pool alone. Each item in a read batch counts toward the cap.
# 0 disables pacing.
MAX_RPC_PER_SECOND=5
# Event runtime omits redundant middleware chain-ID reads for undeclared read
# transactions; explicit Sepolia/declared-chain checks and pacing remain active.
# Catch-up queries both pools together; no new setting is required.
# Optional per-provider compute-unit cap (Alchemy meters CU, not requests:
# eth_getLogs=60, eth_call=26, blocks/txs=20, feeHistory/blockNumber=10).
# The free tier's throughput ceiling is 300 CU/s; 250 leaves headroom. A getLogs
# paces the next call 0.24 s out at 250 CU/s, a blockNumber only 0.04 s.
# 0 disables CU pacing.
MAX_COMPUTE_UNITS_PER_SECOND=250
# Optional: comma-separated fallback RPC URL(s) for when the primary errors (rate limit, outage):
# During startup, use HTTP status codes in eth_getLogs warnings to diagnose plan or quota limits.
# the same request is retried on the next provider, which failed one is then skipped for a minute.
# A re-broadcast sends the identical signed bytes, so it can never double-send. Recommended order:
# keep the provider with the bigger eth_getLogs range (Infura: 10,000 blocks) as RPC_URL, e.g.
# RPC_FALLBACK_URLS=https://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_ALCHEMY_API_KEY
RPC_FALLBACK_URLS=https://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_ALCHEMY_API_KEY
PRIVATE_KEY=
# Phase 2: the loop trades ONLY this primary pool. Prices and profit are f564 per fETH.
POOL_ADDRESS=0x68D8bC396df8e791EFd45A9b214CB07fe4300f48
POOL_FEE=500
# Read-only comparison/research pool; setting it does not enable arbitrage or auto-routing.
# Set the secondary address empty to use only one pool. Fees are hundredths of a basis point.
SECONDARY_POOL_ADDRESS=0xC67Fcb8459C792aE1E271f24CE212Ba90eCC2920
SECONDARY_POOL_FEE=10000
ROUTER_ADDRESS=0x3bFA4769FB09eefC5a80d6E87c3B9C650f7Ae48E
QUOTER_ADDRESS=0xEd1f6473345F45b75F8179591dd5bA1888cf2FB3
F564_ADDRESS=0x21abd410c4240CCC170D004fEfB6405943e0d7Ff
FETH_ADDRESS=0x2238115a9B6469DEE6c004B00A0367fFba2Fd1BC
# enrolled wallet: owns the bot contract and can withdraw
OWNER_ADDRESS=
# One Phase2TradingBot address for every event strategy: docs/EVENT_CONTRACT_SETUP.md.
# Build/prepare with phase2_contract.py, then explicitly deploy, register and fund it.
# Its verification JSON includes required Solidity dependencies for the one deployment.
# main.py runs Phase 2. legacy_main.py runs the original polling bot.
BOT_CONTRACT_ADDRESS=
# Research only: research/2026-11-17-phase2-flow/pending.py uses exported RPC_URL /
# RPC_FALLBACK_URLS (or process-only ALCHEMY_WS_URL) to find an Alchemy Sepolia endpoint.
# It never reads .env: export the connection in the same terminal that launches it.
# It observes pending/mined transactions and never signs or sends trades.
# event_replay.py is offline paper research and does not load .env. Its quote/base caps
# and modeled profit floor are CLI arguments; they do not change vault permissions.
# Front-run/backrun design and input schemas: docs/EVENT_STRATEGIES.md.

# Start Phase 2 with python main.py. Start the loopback dashboard separately.
# Contract deployment/reuse, raw limits, funding and owner calls: docs/EVENT_CONTRACT_SETUP.md.
# Exported variables override file values. --no-env uses exported configuration only.
# Runtime commands use the project's .env, never a parent directory's file.
# Offline build/prepare/replay commands do not load .env. The dashboard reads logs/events/.
# Both Phase 2 charts reuse reconciled snapshots in bounded prices.jsonl.
# activity.json reuses fetched Swap logs / receipts. The runtime diagnostic worker
# makes pinned holdings reads and at most four uncached signer/time lookups per
# 15-second pass; metadata waits while inventory is open. It shares the RPC cap.
# Dashboard tabs/interactions only read local files and add no RPC requests.
# portfolio.json holds actual balances; simulation does not change them.
# No automatic backfill, baseline reset or liquidity scan. Legacy recovery: /legacy.
# Holdings diagnostics continue while awaiting a scan. Recovery checks saved price
# and execution block hashes; open-position entries are checked before every exit.
# Invalidated prices/entry annotations stay withheld. Quota/auth errors do not
# shrink catch-up ranges. These checks require no additional environment settings.
# The main dashboard explains entry blockers; ready/connected does not mean entries started.
# Startup updates a validated logs/events/snapshot.json or the saved Phase 2 research snapshot.
# Scan Pool Liquidity requests one full scan of both pools.
# Missing/invalid layouts block entries with awaiting_scan; recorded exits continue.
# Alchemy Sepolia WSS may be supplied here, or derived from the saved HTTP RPC fallbacks.
# ALCHEMY_WS_URL=wss://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/YOUR_ALCHEMY_KEY
# Older EVENT_EXECUTION_MODE=paper/simulate/live settings are accepted for compatibility.
# Startup always pauses entries in Simulation with a vault, or Paper without one.
# Existing live inventory resumes exit management. After startup select Live (Sepolia)
# and then Start Trading for new live entries. Stop Trading leaves exits running.
# Wait for mode acknowledgement before Start Trading; switching mode invalidates older Start requests.
# The primary row has one Mode toggle and one Start/Stop toggle. Scan/Exit are separate.
# Realized P/L is retained live-ledger coverage, not lifetime or portfolio return.
# Missing trade timestamps hide only the date range, not valid profit/gas totals.
# Dashboard Python updates require a dashboard restart; no setting enables this.
# Without an established portfolio baseline, return stays unavailable.
EVENT_QUOTE_CAP=1000
EVENT_BASE_CAP=10
EVENT_MIN_PROFIT=1
# Maximum output tolerance. Atomic cycles tighten this when needed to retain
# EVENT_MIN_PROFIT; exact output must still reach the floor. Single swaps keep
# their configured tolerance and have no atomic round-trip profit guarantee.
EVENT_SLIPPAGE_BPS=20
EVENT_MOVE_BPS=100
# Offer the atomic backrun cycle while the target transaction is still pending
# (same-block inclusion attempt). No exact quote exists at that moment: the
# integer rung nearest the paper optimum is sent with minimum output
# amount + EVENT_MIN_PROFIT and snapshot-derived price limits; the on-chain
# profit floor rolls back both legs on any failure, so a lost race costs gas
# only. Ordering after the target is not guaranteed; the receipt-confirmed
# backrun path is unchanged. Default ON (contested windows are lost without
# it); set 0/false to restrict backruns to the receipt-confirmed path.
EVENT_PENDING_BACKRUN=true
# Priority fee (tip) in wei for the PENDING backrun cycle ONLY. Default 1,
# one BELOW the market bot's tip=2 group: at our 53-85 ms broadcasts a
# MATCHING tip landed the cycle BEFORE the target itself (block 12193519:
# our tip-2 cycle at idx 210, the target at 212 — a guaranteed pre-target
# revert; competitor-2 made the same mistake one slot later). Any tip below
# the target's group can never sort before it, and tip-1 transactions still
# land same-block right behind the target's group even in the fullest block
# observed (226 txs) — competitor-2 won the 07:30 window exactly this way,
# index 135 directly behind the target. Tip 0 is the old failure (zero-tail,
# a block late). The max fee stays EVENT_MAX_FEE_WEI, so inclusion never
# depends on the tip; every other send keeps EVENT_PRIORITY_FEE_WEI.
EVENT_PENDING_PRIORITY_FEE_WEI=1
# Next-block SWEEP cycle (competitor-1's play), sent in the same ~0.5 s
# window as the same-block cycle: nonce N would carry a 16 gwei cycle whose
# tip tops the block AFTER the target's; nonce N+1 carries the 2-wei
# same-block cycle. DEFAULT OFF since 2026-11-22: it went 0-for-4 live
# (~19 mETH burned) because at our latency the target's block always had
# room — the 16 gwei tip sorted the sweep to index 0 BEFORE the target in
# the target's own block, a guaranteed pre-target revert; the next-block
# prize only exists when that block is already sealed on arrival, which
# never happened. Every capture all week came same-block-behind-target.
# Set true to re-arm the dual submission.
EVENT_PENDING_SWEEP=false
# Sweep tip in wei (1..100 gwei, must fit the fee ceiling). Competitor-1
# wins next-block index 0 with 15 gwei; 16 gwei plus our faster broadcast
# wins the same-tip arrival tie.
EVENT_SWEEP_PRIORITY_FEE_WEI=16000000000
# Second, independent PENDING-tx source raced against the Alchemy feed
# (empty = off). The 2026-11-24 measurement showed a public node
# delivering the same pendings a median ~456 ms before the Alchemy
# subscription pushed them; first arrival wins per hash, and the racer's
# failures never clear readiness or trigger recovery — it is redundancy,
# not a primary. Must be ws:// or wss://.
EVENT_PENDING_RACE_URL=
EVENT_EXIT_SECONDS=60
EVENT_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=10
# Diagnostic-worker cadence in seconds (5..600; default 15). Every pass costs a
# handful of reads: a slower plan trades chart freshness for compute units.
EVENT_OBSERVER_SECONDS=60
EVENT_MAX_GAS=1500000
# Phase 2 fixed bids only (wei/gas; 1 gwei = 1000000000 wei).
# Fixed mode preserves these exact fields, refusing if max < current base + priority.
EVENT_PRIORITY_FEE_WEI=2000000000
EVENT_MAX_FEE_WEI=20000000000
# Opt in with competitive; fixed remains the installation default. Legacy is unaffected.
EVENT_FEE_POLICY=fixed
# Competitive starting tip: 20 gwei, integer wei, 1..100000000000.
EVENT_FEE_BASELINE_WEI=20000000000
# Network multiplier: 12500 bps = 1.25x; integer 10000..50000.
# Network input is the highest p90 reward in eight recent blocks; background refresh >=12s.
EVENT_FEE_NETWORK_BPS=12500
# Relevant configured competitor effective-tip multiplier: 15000 bps = 1.5x; 10000..50000.
EVENT_FEE_COMPETITOR_BPS=15000
# Competitive priority ceiling: 100 gwei; integer wei, baseline..100000000000.
EVENT_FEE_TIP_CAP_WEI=100000000000
# Competitive total fee ceiling: 200 gwei; integer wei, tip cap..200000000000.
# Dynamic max = min(2*base + selected tip, ceiling); reduce tip if needed to cover base.
EVENT_FEE_MAX_CAP_WEI=200000000000
# Competitive ignores both fixed bid fields above; all supplied settings remain validated.
# Missing/stale evidence falls back to baseline with a fresh base-fee check. Caps still apply.
# These are experimental bids, not optimal fees or ordering guarantees. No replacements.
# Formulas, visibility, fallback and public diagnostics: docs/EVENT_FEES.md.
# simulate/live need OWNER_ADDRESS and BOT_CONTRACT_ADDRESS; the Phase 2 ABI ships in abis/.
# Runtime and owner calldata need no Solidity compiler or local build artifact.
# live additionally needs DRY_RUN=false and the operator PRIVATE_KEY. Never use an owner key.
# Remove obsolete ARBITRAGE_VAULT_ADDRESS / EVENT_FRONT_VAULT_ADDRESS settings.
EVENT_MARKET_BOTS=0x6011139d5f16689bde1d245c6247b088f9582026
# Signers that invalidate predictions; competitive fee evidence additionally requires a supported
# official-pool swap. Arbitrary vault/unsupported router calls supply no fee bid; see EVENT_FEES.md.
EVENT_COMPETITORS=0xd067640b2e032b1a878bd87b4d85cb5a87ef64ef,0x21b1a1e9f20377f4cbf6d874cfcf3df37699c7b7
# Entries default paused; backruns on, front-runs and independent cross-pool scan off.
# Enable modules from the Phase 2 dashboard panel; all use the same contract balance.
DRY_RUN=true
# Legacy polling sizing below. Phase 2 uses EVENT_QUOTE_CAP / EVENT_BASE_CAP.
MAX_TRADE_AMOUNT=0.01        # size of one BUY, in f564 (the quote token); a sell closes exactly what the buy delivered. Also the on-chain per-swap cap
# Optional: size each buy by risk instead. A stop loss should cost about this share (%) of the f564 in the vault
# (pool fee and price impact included, worked out on the pool's liquidity layout saved in pool_profile.json,
# The shipped profile is PHASE 1: Phase 2 needs a fresh matching scan before enabling risk sizing.
# Scaled by one read of the pool's liquidity when a buy is sized: a bigger buy pays more impact, and a buy is
# never above MAX_TRADE_AMOUNT. 0 = every buy is MAX_TRADE_AMOUNT.
RISK_PER_TRADE_PCT=0

# --- event watchdog (watchdog.py; alerting only, NEVER restarts/reconciles) ---
# These come from the process environment (systemd unit or export), NOT this
# file: watchdog.py deliberately loads no .env and touches no chain.
# seconds between checks (5..600; default 30)
WATCHDOG_SECONDS=30
# a state.json older than this many seconds means the runtime is dead or
# wedged (15..3600; default 90; the service rewrites state ~once a second)
WATCHDOG_STALE_SECONDS=90
# optional Discord/Slack-style webhook URL receiving one-line alerts
# (halted/failed, awaiting_scan, stale/dead state, recoveries)
WATCHDOG_WEBHOOK_URL=

# --- legacy polling loop (legacy_main.py) ---
# seconds between price checks
POLL_SECONDS=30
# Phase 1 timing feature; leave OFF for Phase 2 (market bot configured for a 45-minute interval).
# Optional: poll this often (seconds) during seconds 30-56 of every minute,
# and every POLL_SECONDS otherwise. 0 = off. A late look at the price costs return (research/2026-11-06-fade-and-exits),
# but this about doubles the requests: check your RPC plan's quota (docs/DEPLOY.md) before using it.
FAST_POLL_SECONDS=0
# minimum seconds between a trade and the next BUY (an exit right after a buy is not delayed).
# This is a Phase 1 strategy default; Phase 2 timing and strategy still need investigation.
COOLDOWN_SECONDS=60
# skip trading (and warn) when the operator wallet has less ETH than this
MIN_OPERATOR_ETH=0.01
# halt the bot after this many failed trades in a row
MAX_TRADE_FAILURES=3

# --- not here ---
# The strategy, take profit, stop loss, the time stop, the pattern-guard limits and the strategy's own numbers are changed on the dashboard
# (python dashboard.py) and saved to logs/control.json. DRY_RUN and MAX_TRADE_AMOUNT can only be
# changed in this file, on purpose, and so can RISK_PER_TRADE_PCT (restart the bot and the dashboard after editing it).
